Introduction
According to the World Health Organization (WHO), the global population of individuals with Parkinson’s Disease (PD) will rise sharply between 12 and 17 million by 2040 [1]. PD is a progressive neurodegenerative disorder characterized by the irreversible loss of neurons in specific regions of the nervous system, resulting in motor dysfunctions [2]. Traditional clinical diagnosis of PD is often time-consuming and resource-intensive, and it frequently falls short of capturing the dynamic and fluctuating symptoms that patients experience in their daily lives. Wearable devices offer a promising solution by enabling continuous, real-time collection of multidimensional physiological data during everyday activities [3]. Analyzing these data through machine learning algorithms allows for effective monitoring and precise diagnosis of PD symptoms [4]. Clinical reviews have highlighted that, although sensitive biomarkers for the early diagnosis of PD are emerging, reliable markers of target engagement and disease modification are still lacking[5]; accordingly, the validation of
