Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs) have shown great potential in addressing data scarcity problems [12]. However, in the medical domain, where generated data must closely align with clinical labels, conventional GANs lack the capacity to generate condition-specific data. Moreover, standard GANs architectures typically rely on convolutional or fully connected networks, causing the generator to capture only partial data distributions and fail to represent the entire data space. Similarly, single-scale discriminators struggle to distinguish between real and synthetic data in the presence of multi-scale features. These limitations make traditional GANs ill-suited for time-series data, as they cannot effectively capture long-term dependencies essential for modeling tremor signals [13]. We propose a GAN-based method for generating PD tremor feature data, which fully leverages the spatiotemporal information embedded in sensor signals. The model learns the latent characteristics of tremor movements and the correlations across different sensor axes to synthesize realistic and meaningful data. The main contributions of this paper are as follows:
(1) We introduce a GAN model designed for multimodal time-series data, named Conditional TremorGANs. By
learning inter-sensor correlations and cross-modal features, the model simulates the dynamic variations of PD patients under different behavioral states, thereby generating sensor data corresponding to various motor conditions. (2) We propose an evaluation method to assess the quality of the synthetic sensor data generated by Conditional TremorGANs. This method verifies the validity of the generated data and improves the performance of PDrelated machine-learning models.
