Related Work
In this section, we review relevant studies in the field. Summarize the application of GANs in sensor data generation and focus on their use in synthesizing data for Parkinson’s Disease patients.
GANs for Sensor Data Generation
GANs have shown significant value in multimodal data processing and human activity recognition. Moustafa et al.[14] proposed a framework called SenseGen, which utilizes a generator composed of multiple Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) networks and a Mixture Density Network (MDN) to synthesize new time-series data from random noise inputs. The discriminator in the SenseGen architecture employs an LSTM network to distinguish between real and synthesized data. Experimental results on smartphone accelerometer data demonstrate that the model generates synthetic samples nearly indistinguishable from real data. These results demonstrate its potential for privacy preservation and offer a deep learning–based framework for future research in synthetic data generation. In most HAR scenarios, sensor data consists of continuous and discrete features. However, traditional GANs are typically limited to modeling continuous variables, which restricts their ability to support HAR model performance. To handle mobile sensor data containing mixed feature types, Joshua et al.[15] introduced HAR-CTGAN. Their method generated more realistic sensor data by incorporating discrete condition settings, Gumbel-Softmax activation,
