GANs for Synthesizing Data in Parkinson’s Disease
Due to the scarcity of data from PD patients, Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs) have become a promising data augmentation technique capable of producing synthetic data that closely resembles real patient data, thereby addressing the problem of data insufficiency. Peppes N et al. [16] employed GANs to tackle the limited availability of freezing of gait (FoG) data in Parkinson’s Disease. They proposed a GAN-based architecture named FoGGAN, designed to generate synthetic FoG data nearly indistinguishable from real patient data. This approach effectively mitigates the challenges of limited data volume and low diversity. In the FoGGAN framework, the generator receives random noise as input and transforms it through multiple neural network layers into synthetic data that mimics the gait characteristics of PD patients. The synthetic data generated by FoGGAN can be used for research and model training, enhancing model performance on unseen data. This method demonstrates strong potential in improving the diversity and quality of available datasets [17]. RafaelAet al. (2023)[18] introduced two novel approaches based on Deep Convolutional GANs (DCGANs) and style transfer to augment electromyographic (EMG) signals of PD patients. Their method utilizes DCGANs to generate simulated EMG tremor patterns by training the generator and discriminator on patient-specific data. Furthermore, they applied tremor signal patterns from PD patients to EMG datasets of healthy individuals, simulating tremor behaviors under different movement protocols. Experimental results confirmed that the proposed methods could adapt to varying frequencies and amplitudes of tremor, accurately replicating individual patient patterns and generalizing them across diverse motion conditions. This work further illustrates the potential of GAN-based methods in synthesizing clinically meaningful data for Parkinson’s Disease research.
Preliminaries
Dataset Description
The dataset used in this study is the Parkinson’s Disease Smartwatch (PADS) dataset provided by Julian Varghese et al.[19] in the Department of Neurology at the University Hospital Münster, Germany. The dataset was collected
over three years, involving 504 participants recruited from a large tertiary care hospital. Participants were categorized into three groups: Parkinson’s Disease patients, differential diagnosis(DD) patients with other movement disorders, and healthy controls(HC). All diagnoses were made by certified neurologists and verified by a senior movement disorder specialist. Advanced DaT scan imaging technology was also used to assist in the diagnosis of cases with uncertain diagnoses. After age matching and data cleaning (including removing erroneous samples and those that did not fit the age distribution), the dataset consisted of 469 valid samples, including 276 PD patients, 114 DD patients, and 79 HC participants. Data were collected using smartwatches and smartphone applications. Participants first completed an electronic questionnaire comprising clinical background (age, height, weight, medications) and 30 items from the PD-specific Non-Motor Symptoms Questionnaire (PDNMS), covering domains such as cognition, sleep, and sexual function. After completing the questionnaire, participants underwent a motor examination under the guidance and supervision of research nurses. During the examination, participants wore two Apple Watch Series 4 smartwatches (one on each wrist). The protocol involved 11 neurologically relevant tasks categorized into rest, posture holding, and movement execution, designed to elicit subtle motor abnormalities. The dataset provides rich multimodal sensor data and detailed annotations, offering valuable support for PD research and the development of intelligent diagnostic models.
