Overview of the Framework
We constructed a unified Conditional TremorGANs model to control the categories and features of the generated data using the Parkinson’s Disease Smartwatch (PADS) dataset provided by Julian Varghese et al. By incorporating conditional information (e.g., patient category labels, medical history features), we can fully explore and utilize the various potential information initially hidden in the sensor data, ensuring the generation of the data diversity. The model introduces a temporal coding module and a temporal self-attention mechanism module [22] in the generator to capture the temporal dependencies in the motion data and generate high-quality data with long-term dependencies. In parallel, a discriminator based on a time-frequency enhancement module and a multi-scale feature extraction module are introduced to ensure that the frequency domain features of the generated data are consistent with the real data, thus enhancing the performance of the discriminator. We employ three different neural network architectures as foundational modules to accommodate the three distinct task categories in patient activities. This ensures adaptability to the diverse movement patterns observed in Parkinson’s patients. To mitigate potential instability caused by unequal update frequencies between the discriminator and the generator, we adopt a fixed update ratio nD : nG = 1 : 1and regularization(gradient penalty). Independently, the generator receives a standard latent input z ∼ N (0, I) to promote sample diversity. The pseudo-code of the Conditional TremorGANs model is shown in Algorithm 1.
