The effectiveness of the conditional component was evaluated in two ways. First, a classifier trained on real data was applied to synthetic sequences, achieving an average accuracy of 96.8% and a macro-F1 of 0.94. These results are close to the scores obtained on real data (98.1% and 0.95), confirming that the generator preserves the intended conditional information. Second, distributional alignment between real and synthetic data was assessed. The average KL divergence between real and generated conditional distributions was 0.021, and the real–synthetic separability AUC was 0.52, indicating that the conditional strata of generated samples are nearly indistinguishable from those of real data. Figure 9 illustrates that t-SNE embeddings of synthetic sequences form clusters overlapping closely with their real counterparts. The impact of these generation strategies on downstream PD classification was also examined. The baseline using only real data achieved an accuracy of 87.4% and a macro-F1 of 0.842. Incorporating per-sample augmentation increased performance to 89.2% accuracy and 0.861 macro-F1. The new-individual synthesis strategy produced the best results, with accuracy reaching 90.6% and macro-F1 improving to 0.876. Gains were most pronounced in postural tasks, where inter-individual variability is higher; macro-F1 increased by 5.1 points compared with the baseline. These findings demonstrate that Conditional TremorGANs not only maintain conditional fidelity but also enhance subject-level diversity, which is crucial for generalization in PD data analysis.
