Synthetic Data Generation and Conditional Evaluation
In Parkinson’s disease datasets, the primary limitation lies in the scarcity of subject-level variability rather than the number of samples per individual. To address this, we refined the synthetic-data protocol and conducted a systematic evaluation of the conditional component in Conditional TremorGANs. This evaluation clarifies the role of the conditional vector, its distributional modeling, and its ability to generate new individuals beyond the observed dataset. We adopted two complementary generation strategies. In the first strategy, which served as our main experimental setting, the training set was enlarged by a factor of three. For each real training instance, three synthetic counterparts were produced using the observed conditional vector c (e.g., resting, postural, or kinetic task). This resulted in a synthetic-to-real ratio of 3:1. Synthetic sequences were created exclusively for training subjects and were never produced for validation or test participants, thereby preserving the subject-wise split and preventing any leakage. The second strategy was designed to enrich inter-subject variability by creating data corresponding to virtual individuals. In this setting, the empirical distribution of conditional vectors in the training set was estimated, and Gaussian mixture models were fitted to capture their variability. New conditional vectors were then sampled from these models and combined with Gaussian noise to drive the generator. This procedure produced a global synthetic pool equal in size to that of the per-sample augmentation strategy (three times the number of real training samples). By sampling from the modeled
